    "In an isosceles triangle, the two base angles are equal, and the vertex angle is 88°. What does each base angle measure?": "The two base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal, and all three angles add up to 180. Subtract the vertex angle first: 180 minus 88 is 92. Split that evenly between the two equal base angles: 92 divided by 2 is 46 degrees each.",
    "Angle A and Angle B are complementary. If Angle A is 5 times Angle B, what is Angle B?": "Complementary angles add up to 90 degrees. Since A is 5 times B, replace A with 5B: 5B plus B is 6B, and that has to equal 90. Divide 90 by 6, and B is 15 degrees.",
    "A ladder leans against a wall, forming a right triangle with the ground. The ladder (hypotenuse) is 25 ft, and the base is 7 ft from the wall. How high up the wall does the ladder reach?": "Use the Pythagorean theorem with the height as the missing leg: leg squared plus height squared equals hypotenuse squared. 49 plus height squared equals 625, so height squared is 625 minus 49, which is 576. The square root of 576 is 24, so the height is 24 feet.",
